    {
      "source": "hackernews",
      "subsource": "news.ycombinator.com",
      "category": "ai",
      "title": "OpenAI might have stolen another major proof",
      "url": "https://twitter.com/ValerioCapraro/status/2097791836269977996",
      "discuss_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49638353",
      "score": 237,
      "comments": 121,
      "author": "tamnd",
      "published": "2026-09-10T04:19:48Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Controversy surrounds OpenAI reportedly claiming credit for mathematical proofs without citing prior work from independent academic researchers.",
        "The dispute highlights attribution vulnerabilities and IP provenance risks when frontier reasoning models ingest pre-print research and formal verification code.",
        "Teams relying on LLMs for scientific reasoning must independently verify originality and prior art before asserting novelty or production viability."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "Why Function Arguments Are Not Function Colors",
      "url": "https://jerf.org/iri/post/2026/func_args_are_not_colors/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/tsfs3w",
      "score": 21,
      "comments": 11,
      "author": "abhin4v",
      "published": "2026-09-09T04:13:05Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Function coloring virally splits execution runtimes, whereas explicit arguments preserve standard calling conventions and synchronous call stack semantics.",
        "Passing parameters like allocators or contexts avoids language bifurcation, enabling straightforward wrapping, mocking, and caller-controlled composition.",
        "Unlike async/await mechanics, function arguments never restrict callers' execution environments or mandate pervasive ecosystem-wide refactoring."
      ]
    },
